    "body": "Agility Robotics , the maker of the Digit humanoid robot , began trading on Nasdaq on Thursday after closing its merger with Churchill Capital Corp XI. The company said the deal and related financing raised about $465 million before transaction expenses. Its shares trade under AGLT, and its warrants trade under AGLTW.\nWhat’s Related\nThe Salem, Oregon, company plans to use the money to fill customer orders , build more robots, and put Digit to work at more sites. The total includes about $164 million from Churchill’s trust account, $201 million from a private investment led by Foxconn, and $100 million in equity funding Agility received before the merger closed on Nov. 4.\nAgility says it’s the first U.S. publicly listed company focused solely on humanoid robots to have its machines in commercial use. “Digit is doing real work in customer operations today, taking on repetitive, physically demanding tasks,” said Agility Robotics CEO Peggy Johnson.\nRELATED: Agility Robotics Unveils Digit 5 With Heavier Lift, Faster Charge\nGXO expands usage of Digit\nAgility’s robots have logged more than 65,000 hours of work at customer sites over nearly three years. Current customers include GXO, Schaeffler and Toyota Motor Manufacturing Canada. At GXO’s Flowery Branch, Georgia, facility, Digit has moved more than 100,000 totes since the companies signed a commercial agreement in 2024.\nGXO plans to add Digit at sites in Statesville, North Carolina, and Lebanon, Tennessee, in early 2027. The agreement includes Agility Arc, cloud software that manages the robots and integrates them into customer operations. Agricultural equipment maker AGCO also signed an agreement for a pilot expected to start in 2027.\nThree more manufacturers are paying to test Digit on specific jobs: a major global automaker, a large automotive parts developer, and a consumer products maker. The company says it’s also talking with more than 30 prospective customers.\nDigit 5 will be widely available by 2028\nAgility says it has more than $300 million in committed multiyear orders for Digit 5, the model it unveiled in September. The value of those orders depends on Agility meeting contract milestones and delivering the agreed features and specifications. The company expects early customers to get Digit 5 in the first half of 2027 and aims to make it widely available by year-end.\nAgility designed Digit 5 to repeatedly lift up to 50 pounds and work closer to people. It can use different tools at the ends of its arms depending on the job. Agility says a Nyobolt battery recharges in nine minutes, which could give Digit 5 more than 20 hours of working time per day. The company plans to use NVIDIA’s IGX Thor computer in Digit 5 and expand its use of NVIDIA’s Halos safety technology .\nAgility plans to build more robots at RoboFab, its 70,000-square-foot factory in Salem. The company says it designed the plant to produce up to 10,000 robots a year. It’s also adding a 50,000-square-foot site in Pittsburgh for software, AI , and customer support.",
